  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study analyzes the relationship between the gender of household heads and three housing asset indicators in China: primary homeownership, second homeownership, and housing asset value, while also testing if having a son alters these dynamics. The empirical analysis reveals that while male household heads have a higher probability of primary homeownership, female household heads are more likely to own a second home and female-headed households hold higher-value housing assets than their male counterparts.",
      "why": "The findings challenge the assumption of uniform male advantage in real estate accumulation by demonstrating that gender disparities in housing wealth operate differently across primary ownership, secondary property investment, and asset valuation."
    }
